  field defaults to "use global," so nothing stored today has to change.

  Worth noting these are cheap to change [3mexcept[0m per-task, which once shipped means stored tasks carry an offset field
  you have to keep migrating.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What rule decides when a reminder fires? → Global lead time + overdue (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I present the design. The repo has no tooling at all — no package manifest, no linter, no test
  runner. Setting these up is cheapest now, before there's any code to retrofit.

  Relevant to this feature specifically: the reminder engine is pure time arithmetic — "has this crossed its lead-time
  boundary, and have I already fired for it?" That's exactly the kind of logic that's miserable to verify by hand (you'd
  be waiting an hour to see if a reminder fires) and trivial to unit test with an injected clock. I'd consider a test
  runner close to non-negotiable here, and I'll design the scheduler to take a clock as a parameter so it's testable at
  all.

  Note this does introduce a build/dev dependency on Node + npm for tooling, though the app itself stays dependency-free
  vanilla JS that runs by opening the file.
